/*
SERIES: Vibecode Town — Season 1
EPISODE: 02 — "Spec-Driven Dev가 뭔대" / "What Is Spec-Driven Dev?"
VERSION: v6.0 (series reframe)
SEGMENT STRUCTURE: 6-segment (HOOK → MISCONCEPTION → THE_CRACK → CORE → REFRAME → OUTRO_CTA)
TARGET DURATION: 3:30-3:55
NARRATOR: English V.O. only. Vee is SILENT.
SSOT: systems/video/SERIES_BIBLE.md
*/

/* SEGMENT 1: HOOK [0:00-0:15] */
# visual_mode: A
# characters: vee
# shorts_candidate: true
# space: desk

= Synopsis: EP01 recap — Vee learned vibe coding = collaboration. Now she tries it for real. Five attempts, five different results.

INT. VEE'S DESK - DAY

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Last time, Vee learned the first rule of vibe coding: you decide what, AI decides how.

= Visual: Quick flash — EP01 ending. Vee's small smile. Sticky note on monitor.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
So she tried it. Five times. Five prompts. Same project.

= Visual: Screen splits into five panels. Each shows a completely different result. Different colors, different layouts, different features. Vee stares.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Five completely different things came back.

= Vee expression: mouth slightly open. Not angry. Bewildered.

/* SEGMENT 2: MISCONCEPTION [0:15-0:45] */
# visual_mode: A
# characters: vee
# shorts_candidate: false
# space: desk

= Synopsis: "Be more specific" — Vee writes longer and longer prompts. Results get WORSE, not better.

INT. VEE'S DESK - CONTINUOUS

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Okay, she thought. Maybe I wasn't specific enough. So she got specific.

= Visual: Vee typing. The prompt grows. And grows. Camera slowly zooms out — the text extends beyond the screen, scrolling and scrolling.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
"Build me an online store with a blue header, three product categories, a shopping cart on the right side, login button top-left, use a modern font, make it responsive, add a search bar..."

= Visual: Prompt text fills the entire screen. Tiny font. Endless words.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The result?

= Visual: The AI output appears. It's... worse. The header is blue but covers the entire page. Three categories exist but overlap each other. The cart is on the right — of a different page.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Worse. More detail didn't help. It made things more confusing — for the AI. Because detail is not the same as direction.

= Vee slumps back in chair. Hands drop from keyboard.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
More words. More mess. She was giving the AI a thousand instructions but zero purpose.

= Bee on monitor edge, holding tiny placard: "WHERE ARE WE GOING?"

/* SEGMENT 3: THE_CRACK [0:45-1:15] */
# visual_mode: C
# characters: []
# shorts_candidate: true
# space: screen

= Synopsis: The numbers. 10,847 lines. 4,200 lines of duplication in red. Five agents, zero coordination.

INT. SCREEN SPACE - CONTINUOUS

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Let's look at what actually happened inside the code.

= Visual: A counter starts at zero. Rapidly climbs. Numbers rolling like a slot machine.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Ten thousand, eight hundred, forty-seven lines of code.

= Visual: Counter stops at 10,847. Big number. Holds for a beat.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Almost half of them — four thousand two hundred lines — were duplicates.

= Visual: The counter splits. 6,647 stays white. 4,200 turns red and visually separates — red blocks pulling away from white blocks. The gap is visceral.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Five AI sessions built the same functions five different ways. Three date formatters. Two config parsers. Four ways to validate the same input. None of them knew about the others.

= Visual: Five small robot icons appear, each in their own box. No lines connecting them. Each building something slightly different. Isolated.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Five construction crews on the same lot. No blueprint. No foreman. Just... build.

= Visual: Five tiny buildings, all different styles, crammed onto one lot. Leaning against each other. Unstable.

/* SEGMENT 4: CORE [1:15-3:00] */
# visual_mode: B
# characters: []
# shorts_candidate: false
# space: whiteboard

= Synopsis: Building metaphor (25s compressed) → 3-line spec definition → Before/After demo (30s) → CLAUDE.md screen (15s) → spec-kit intro (15s)

INT. WHITEBOARD SPACE - CONTINUOUS

= Visual: A construction site. No blueprints visible. Workers stacking blocks randomly.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Imagine building a house without a blueprint. You know you want a house. You start stacking bricks. Some go left, some go right. Eventually something stands up. But the bathroom is on the roof and there's no front door.

= Visual: Chaotic building — bricks stacked randomly. Bathroom fixtures on top. No entrance. Building wobbles.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Now imagine one piece of paper. Not a hundred-page document. One note. "Two bedrooms. One bathroom. Front door faces south."

= Visual: A simple note card appears. Three lines of text. Clean. Small. The building re-assembles — this time in order. Foundation, walls, roof. Door faces the right way.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Same workers. Same bricks. Completely different result. That note is a spec.

= Visual: Building stands solid. Simple but correct.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And a spec doesn't have to be complicated. Three lines is enough.

= Visual: Template appears on whiteboard, building line by line:

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Line one: Goal. What are you building? "An online store that sells handmade candles."

= Visual: "Goal: An online store that sells handmade candles" appears.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Line two: Constraints. What are you NOT building? "No user accounts. No payment processing — just a catalog with a contact form."

= Visual: "Constraints: No accounts, no payments. Catalog + contact form only." appears below.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Line three: Done-when. How do you know it's finished? "When someone can browse candles and send a message."

= Visual: "Done-when: Browse candles + send message" appears. Three lines. Clean.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
That's it. Three lines. Write these before you write a single prompt.

= Visual: The three lines glow briefly. Then transition.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Now watch what happens. Same project. Without a spec — five sessions, five different stores.

= Visual: Left panel — five chaotic results, all different. Messy. Arrows pointing everywhere.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
With a spec — first attempt is close. Second attempt nails it. Twenty minutes instead of three hours.

= Visual: Right panel — two clean results, similar to each other. Convergent. Green checkmarks.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The spec didn't make AI smarter. It made your instructions clearer.

= Visual: Before/After side by side. Clear visual difference. Hold for 3 seconds.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And there are tools that make this even easier. CLAUDE dot MD — a file that lives in your project. Every time Claude opens your code, it reads this file first. Your rules. Your constraints. Your context. Persistent.

= Visual: Screen space — a file tree. CLAUDE.md highlighted. Content visible: project rules, constraints, tech stack. Clean dark-mode editor.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Cursor has the same idea — dot cursor rules. Write your rules once, every AI session follows them.

= Visual: .cursorrules file in Cursor IDE. Similar concept, different tool.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And if you want a full workflow — GitHub's spec-kit. Four steps: specify, plan, tasks, implement. Each step validates before moving to the next.

= Visual: Four-step flow diagram. Specify → Plan → Tasks → Implement. Arrows between them. Checkmarks at each gate.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The point isn't which tool. The point is: write down what you want before you ask AI to build it.

/* SEGMENT 5: REFRAME [3:00-3:30] */
# visual_mode: B
# characters: []
# shorts_candidate: false
# space: whiteboard

= Synopsis: "Frustration IS the spec." Your annoyance, organized, becomes the blueprint.

INT. WHITEBOARD SPACE - CONTINUOUS

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Here's the part nobody tells you. That frustration you felt when AI made the wrong thing? When the store was neon pink and the login went to the cart?

= Visual: Flashback — the neon pink store from MISCONCEPTION. Quick flash.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
That's not failure. That's data.

= Visual: The frustration — represented as messy red scribbles — transforms. The scribbles organize themselves into neat lines. Red becomes blue. Chaos becomes structure.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Write down what annoyed you. "It should have done X but did Y." Congratulations. You just wrote a requirement. Organize your frustrations, and you've got a spec.

= Visual: A notepad. Scribbled complaints morph into the three-line spec template. Goal. Constraints. Done-when.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Frustration is the spec. Your annoyance, organized, is the blueprint.

/* SEGMENT 6: OUTRO_CTA [3:30-3:55] */
# visual_mode: A
# characters: vee
# shorts_candidate: false
# space: desk

= Synopsis: 3-line spec template on screen. Vee writes her first spec. "Next time you ask AI to build something, write these three lines first."

INT. VEE'S DESK - DAY

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Here's your homework. Next time — before you type a single prompt — grab a piece of paper. Or open a blank file. And write three lines.

= Visual: The three-line template appears on screen, large and clear:
= "Goal: ___"
= "Constraints: ___"
= "Done-when: ___"

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Goal. Constraints. Done-when. Fill in the blanks. Then give it to the AI.

= Visual: Vee at desk. She has the template on her monitor. She starts writing — pen on sticky note, filling in three lines. Quick, confident strokes.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
You'll be surprised how different the result is when AI knows where it's going.

= Visual: Vee sticks the note to her monitor. Turns to type a prompt. Something appears on screen. She looks at it. Looks at her spec. Looks back at the screen. Nods — not perfect, but close.

= Bee appears briefly on monitor corner, antenna up — approving stance. 0.5 seconds.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Next time — how do you actually read the code AI writes? Even if you've never coded before. See you then.

= Visual: Vee's monitor with the result + the spec note side by side. Fade to end card.

= Vee small smile. Cut to black.

> END OF EPISODE 02
